The '''DeKalb Avenue station''' is an interchange [[metro station|station]] on the [[BMT Brighton Line]] and [[BMT Fourth Avenue Line]] of the [[New York City Subway]], located at the intersection of [[DeKalb Avenue|DeKalb]] and [[Flatbush Avenue]]s in [[Downtown Brooklyn]]. It is served by the [[Q (New York City Subway service)|Q]] and [[R (New York City Subway service)|R]] trains at all times, the [[B (New York City Subway service)|B]] train on weekdays, and the [[D (New York City Subway service)|D]] and [[N (New York City Subway service)|N]] trains during late nights. During rush hours only, a few [[W (New York City Subway service)|W]] train trips also serve this station.

The station has six tracks and two [[island platforms]]. The two innermost tracks are used by Fourth Avenue express trains, which skip the station, while the four outer tracks are used by Brighton Line and Fourth Avenue local trains. The track configuration has been arranged several times. The current configuration dates from a 1956–1961 reconstruction project to straighten the platforms and eliminate a [[level junction]] north of the station, which had caused a switching bottleneck. Other plans, such as connections to the [[BMT Fulton Street Line]] and [[IRT Eastern Parkway Line]], were considered at times.

== History ==

=== Construction and opening ===
The [[New York City Board of Estimate]] approved the construction of the [[BMT Fourth Avenue Line|Fourth Avenue Line]], a subway line running under [[Fourth Avenue (Brooklyn)|Fourth Avenue]], in early 1908. The line would run over the [[Manhattan Bridge]] to [[Manhattan]] at its northern end.<ref>{{Cite news |date=June 21, 1911 |title=Building of Fourth Ave. Subway Crowns Decade of Civic Struggle |language=en |page=10 |work=The Brooklyn Daily Eagle |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-daily-eagle-building-of-fou/125299739/ |access-date=May 4, 2017 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref><ref name="Public Service Commission 19152">{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XoxBAQAAMAAJ&q=4th+Avenue+line&pg=PA18 |title=Fourth Avenue Subway, Brooklyn's New Transportation Line: A Part of the Dual System of Rapid Transit of the City of New York |date=June 19, 1915 |publisher=New York State Public Service Commission |language=en}}</ref> Contracts for the Fourth Avenue Line were awarded on May 22, 1908, for the section between 43rd Street and the Manhattan Bridge, but the Board of Estimate did not approve them until October 29, 1909, when a taxpayer's lawsuit regarding the city's debt was settled.<ref name="Public Service Commission 19152"/> Groundbreaking for the first section of the subway, between DeKalb Avenue and 43rd Street (ending at [[36th Street (BMT Fourth Avenue Line)|36th Street]]), took place on November 13, 1909, at [[DeKalb Avenue]] and [[Flatbush Avenue]], after the plans and surveys for the line were completed.<ref name="NYTImes-BMT4Av-DirtFly-1909">{{cite news |date=November 14, 1909 |title=Fourth Ave. Subway Dirt Begins to Fly |work=The New York Times |url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1909/11/14/101031065.pdf |url-status=live |access-date=June 28, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210810055920/https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1909/11/14/101031065.pdf |archive-date=August 10, 2021}}</ref><ref name="n1455782">{{Cite news |date=June 19, 1915 |title=Opening of Fourth Avenue Subway Marks Climax of Eleven Years' Work |pages=24 |work=The Brooklyn Daily Eagle |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-daily-eagle-opening-of-four/1455782/ |access-date=May 27, 2023}}</ref> The Fourth Avenue Line was assigned to the BRT (after 1923, the [[Brooklyn–Manhattan Transit Corporation]] or BMT<ref name="New York (State). Transit Commission 1924 p. 501">{{cite book | title=State of New York Transit Commission Third Annual Report for the Calendar Year 1923| publisher=New York State Transit Commission | issue=v. 3 | year=1924 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lFxLAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA501 | page=501}}</ref>) in the [[Dual Contracts]], adopted on March 4, 1913.<ref name="Walker 1918">{{cite book |last1=Walker |first1=James Blaine |url=https://archive.org/details/fiftyyearsrapid00walkgoog |title=Fifty Years of Rapid Transit — 1864 to 1917 |date=1918 |publisher=Law Printing |location=New York, N.Y. |access-date=November 6, 2016}}</ref>{{Rp|203–219}}<ref name="Engineering News 1910">{{cite journal |date=March 10, 1910 |title=A New Subway Line for New York City |url=http://www.nycsubway.org/articles/triboro.html |url-status=live |journal=Engineering News |volume=63 |issue=10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120705042944/http://www.nycsubway.org/articles/triboro.html |archive-date=July 5, 2012 |access-date=December 25, 2020}}</ref><ref name="nyt-1913-03-20">{{Cite news |date=March 20, 1913 |title=Subway Contracts Solemnly Signed; Cheers at the Ceremonial Function When McCall Gets Willcox to Attest. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1913/03/20/100611839.pdf |url-status=live |access-date=January 11, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220504021115/https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1913/03/20/100611839.pdf |archive-date=May 4, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> William Bradley built the portion of the line from the intersection of Flatbush Avenue Extension and Willoughby Street to the intersection of Fulton Street and Ashland Place, including the DeKalb Avenue station.<ref name="nyt-1911-08-27">{{Cite news |date=August 27, 1911 |title=Rapid Subway Work on Brooklyn Line; More Than Half the Fourth Ave. Contract Completed and 80 Per Cent. of Excavation. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1911/08/27/archives/rapid-subway-work-on-brooklyn-line-more-than-half-the-fourth-ave.html |url-status=live |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521184918/https://www.nytimes.com/1911/08/27/archives/rapid-subway-work-on-brooklyn-line-more-than-half-the-fourth-ave.html |archive-date=May 21, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The DeKalb Avenue station was originally intended only as a local station on the Fourth Avenue Line. After the [[Montague Street Tunnel]] was approved,<ref name="n125406642">{{Cite news |date=November 10, 1914 |title=Will Reconstruct DeKalb Ave. Station |pages=4 |work=The Brooklyn Daily Eagle |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-daily-eagle-will-reconstruc/125406642/ |access-date=May 27, 2023}}</ref> the planned station was changed to an express station that could serve trains from both the Manhattan Bridge and the Montague Street Tunnel (there would still be two bypass tracks for Fourth Avenue express trains using the bridge).<ref name="n1455782" /><ref name="n125407342">{{Cite news |date=June 19, 1915 |title=Brooklyn Rejoices Over New Subway |pages=1, [https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-brooklyn-rejoices-ove/125407342/ 3] |work=The Standard Union |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-brooklyn-rejoices-ove/125407255/ |access-date=May 27, 2023}}</ref> In November 1914, the New York Public Service Commission began requesting bids for the installation of station finishes at the DeKalb Avenue station.<ref name="n125406642" /><ref name="p129459634">{{cite news |date=November 30, 1914 |title=Receiving Bids for New Subway Construction: Contracts for Section No.4 of Broadway Subway and for Dekalb Avenue Station in Brooklyn Make Total of 66 Sections Out of $3 for the System |page=5 |work=The Wall Street Journal |issn=0099-9660 |id={{ProQuest|129459634}}}}</ref> The commission also requested bids for the addition of crossovers, allowing trains from the tunnel or the bridge to stop at DeKalb Avenue.<ref name="n125406642" /> The Public Service Commission awarded the contract for the modifications to Samuel Beskin.<ref name="n125407426">{{Cite news |date=February 5, 1915 |title=Bond for Station Change Approved |pages=2 |work=The Standard Union |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-bond-for-station-chan/125407426/ |access-date=May 27, 2023}}</ref> To accommodate these crossovers, contractors had to cut into partition walls that had already been built.<ref name="n125407342" />

The DeKalb Avenue station opened on June 22, 1915, as part of an extension of the subway to [[Coney Island–Stillwell Avenue station|Coney Island]], which included the Fourth Avenue Line north of [[59th Street (BMT Fourth Avenue Line)|59th Street]] as well as the entire [[BMT Sea Beach Line|Sea Beach Line]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Cudahy |first=Brian J. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=cJGUDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA218 |title=How We Got to Coney Island: The Development of Mass Transportation in Brooklyn and Kings County |publisher=Fordham University Press |year=2009 |isbn=978-0-8232-2211-7 |pages=217–218 |access-date=April 24, 2020}}</ref><ref name="Station Opening">{{cite web |date=June 22, 1915 |title=Through Tube to Coney, 48 Minutes: First Train on Fourth Avenue Route Beats West End Line Eleven Minutes |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/48814840/ |access-date=June 29, 2015 |publisher=[[Brooklyn Daily Eagle]] |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> The station's opening was marked with a competition between two trains heading from [[Chambers Street station (BMT Nassau Street Line)|Chambers Street station]] in Manhattan to the Coney Island station, one heading via the [[BMT West End Line|West End Line]] and the other via the Sea Beach Line; the latter got to Coney Island first.<ref name="Station Opening" /> As a local station, the DeKalb Avenue station was originally {{Convert|435|ft|m}} long.<ref name="Public Service Commission 19152"/>{{Rp|page=24}}<ref name="E. Croswell 1916">{{Cite book |last=Assembly |first=New York (State) Legislature |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=j6k1NdhtMPAC&pg=PA855 |title=Documents of the Assembly of the State of New York |date=1916 |publisher=E. Croswell |pages=849–856 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=854}}

Also as part of the Dual Contracts, an extension of the [[BMT Brighton Line|Brighton Line]] was approved.<ref name="n125097279">{{Cite news |date=April 13, 1913 |title=Transit Relief Big Stimulus |pages=13, [https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-citizen-transit-relief-will/125097279/ 14] |work=The Brooklyn Citizen |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-citizen-transit-relief-big/125097305/ |url-status=live |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230522001408/https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-citizen-transit-relief-big/125097305/ |archive-date=May 22, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> The extension was to run under St. Felix Street in Downtown Brooklyn, splitting off the Fourth Avenue Line south of the DeKalb Avenue station. It would then run under Flatbush Avenue south of [[Atlantic Avenue (New York City)|Atlantic Avenue]], sharing a tunnel with the [[Interborough Rapid Transit Company]] (IRT)'s [[IRT Eastern Parkway Line|Eastern Parkway Line]].<ref name="p575088610">{{cite news |date=May 4, 1913 |title=Dual Subway Stations: Protesting Owners Should File Petitions for Changes |page=C8 |work=New-York Tribune |issn=1941-0646 |id={{ProQuest|575088610}}}}</ref><ref name="nyt-1913-07-06">{{Cite news |date=July 6, 1913 |title=Station Sites for New Subways; Pamphlet Issued by Utilities Board Contains List of Stops on Dual System. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1913/07/06/archives/station-sites-for-new-subways-pamphlet-issued-by-utilities-board.html |url-status=live |access-date=May 20, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230213035017/https://www.nytimes.com/1913/07/06/archives/station-sites-for-new-subways-pamphlet-issued-by-utilities-board.html |archive-date=February 13, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The Brighton Line extension opened on August 1, 1920,<ref name="n125080164">{{Cite news |date=August 1, 1920 |title=Brooklyn and Queens Brought Closer to Manhattan and Its Activities by New Subway Transit Links |pages=32 |work=New-York Tribune |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/new-york-tribune-brooklyn-and-queens-bro/125080164/ |url-status=live |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521185801/https://www.newspapers.com/article/new-york-tribune-brooklyn-and-queens-bro/125080164/ |archive-date=May 21, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> serving the DeKalb Avenue station with the Fourth Avenue Line and providing direct service between the existing Brighton Line and [[Midtown Manhattan]].<ref name="bde19200725">{{Cite news |date=July 25, 1920 |title=New Subways Add Seven More Miles to BRT on Aug 1 |work=[[Brooklyn Daily Eagle]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/55629628/?terms=brt%2Bsubway%2Bmontague |url-status=live |access-date=August 19, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161026232118/https://www.newspapers.com/image/55629628/?terms=brt%2Bsubway%2Bmontague |archive-date=October 26, 2016 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> Afterward, the DeKalb Avenue station was referred to as "the heart of the BMT", as it is a major transfer point between BMT services with lines splitting north and south of the station.<ref>{{cite web |title=Did You Know? DeKalb Avenue station is known as the "heart of the BMT". |url=https://twitter.com/nytransitmuseum/status/1036622526699515904 |access-date=July 13, 2019 |publisher=NY Transit Museum |via=[[Twitter]]}}</ref>

=== 1920s to 1950s ===
[[File:DeKalb Avenue's HEET Turnstiles.jpg|250px|thumb|left|HEET turnstiles]]
In 1922, the New York State Transit Commission directed its engineers to prepare plans for lengthening the platforms at 23 stations on the BMT's lines to accommodate eight-car trains. As part of the project, platforms would be lengthened to {{Convert|530|feet|meters}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Legislature |first=New York (State) |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-ugaAQAAIAAJ&q=%22union+street%22+LENGTHENING&pg=RA3-PA100 |title=Second Annual Report of the Transit Commission (For the Calendar Year 1922) |date=1923 |publisher=New York State Transit Commission |pages=100 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=u5kT80_w6rYC&q=%22union+street%22+%22530+feet%22&pg=PA1277 |title=Proceedings of the Transit Commission, State of New York Volume III From January 1 to December 31, 1923 |date=1923 |publisher=New York State Transit Commission |pages=1277 |language=en}}</ref> Though the Transit Commission ordered the BMT to lengthen these platforms in September 1923,<ref name="n125208361">{{Cite news |date=December 5, 1927 |title=B.M.T. Prepared for Eight-Car Train Service |pages=2 |work=The Standard Union |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-bmt-prepared-for-e/125208361/ |url-status=live |access-date=May 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230524010137/https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-bmt-prepared-for-e/125208361/ |archive-date=May 24, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> no further progress was made until February 16, 1925, when the [[New York City Board of Transportation]] (BOT) commissioned its engineers to examine platform-lengthening plans for DeKalb Avenue and eleven other stations along the Fourth Avenue Line. It estimated the project would cost $633,000 ({{inflation|index=US|value=633000|start_year=1925|r=-3|fmt=eq}}).<ref>{{Cite news |date=February 17, 1925 |title=12 B-M. T. Stations To Be Lengthened; Transportation Board Orders Engineers to Prepare Contracts for Brooklyn Work. Cost Put At $633,000 Letter to Commission Urges That Company Be Compelled to Buy New Cars. |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1925/02/17/archives/12-bm-t-stations-to-be-lengthehed-transportation-board-orders.html |url-status=live |access-date=May 4, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190821070631/https://www.nytimes.com/1925/02/17/archives/12-bm-t-stations-to-be-lengthehed-transportation-board-orders.html |archive-date=August 21, 2019 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The [[New York City Board of Estimate]] appropriated $362,841 for the lengthening of the platforms at DeKalb Avenue and five other stations in January 1926<ref name="p1112705635">{{cite news |date=January 23, 1926 |title=City to Spend $362,841 On Six B.-M.T. Platforms: Board of Estimate Appropriates Fund to Lengthen Stations in Brooklyn |page=2 |work=The New York Herald, New York Tribune |issn=1941-0646 |id={{ProQuest|1112705635}}}}</ref><ref name="n125208270">{{Cite news |date=January 4, 1926 |title=Brooklyn's Share of Many Millions for Subways Small |pages=10 |work=The Standard Union |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-brooklyns-share-of-m/125208270/ |access-date=May 23, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> and awarded the contract to Charles Meads & Company early the next month.<ref name="New York (N.Y.). Board of Transportation 1926 p. 239">{{cite book |author=New York (N.Y.). Board of Transportation |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fHQcAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA239 |title=Proceedings |publisher=The Board. |year=1926 |page=239 |access-date=May 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230523234210/https://books.google.com/books?id=fHQcAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA239 |archive-date=May 23, 2023 |url-status=live |issue=v. 4}}</ref><ref name="n125208462">{{Cite news |date=February 8, 1926 |title=Snow Holding Up Platform Lengthening on the B-M.T.; Get More Bids February 25 |pages=2 |work=The Standard Union |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-standard-union-snow-holding-up-platf/125208462/ |access-date=May 23, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> As part of the project, the original signal tower at the end of the platform was demolished to make way for the platform extension, and a new signal tower was built to control traffic on the Manhattan Bridge and Montague Street Tunnel.<ref name="p11137040922">{{cite news |date=August 2, 1927 |title=B. M, T. Station Lengthening Is Nearly Finished: 76 Platforms Are Extended 3,186 Feet to Make Room for 126.000 Additional Passengers in Rush Hours City Carried Out Work I.R.T. Changes Planned, but That Company Refuses to Pay Its Share of Costs |page=32 |work=New York Herald Tribune |issn=1941-0646 |id={{ProQuest|1113704092}}}}</ref> The platform extension at DeKalb Avenue opened on August 1, 1927.<ref name="SU-Opens-1927">{{cite news |date=August 1, 1927 |title=B.M.T. Stations Ready For Eight-Car Trains |page=1 |work=Brooklyn Standard Union |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/49706640/ |access-date=April 9, 2020 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref><ref name="p11137040922" />

The [[Dime Savings Bank of New York|Dime Savings Bank]], which had its headquarters at [[Dime Savings Bank Building|9 DeKalb Avenue]] just above the station, opened a branch on the station's mezzanine level in August 1956.<ref name="nyt-1956-08-29">{{Cite news |date=August 29, 1956 |title=Dime Savings Sets Saturday Opening For Sidewalk Unit; Subway Unit Planned |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1956/08/29/archives/dime-savings-sets-saturday-opening-for-sidewalk-unit-subway-unit.html |access-date=May 29, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The opening of the bank branch followed several months of negotiations. The bank had wanted to open a corridor from the station directly into its building, but this was not possible because of the presence of various underground conduits, pipes, and emergency exits, so the bank decided instead to rent space in the station.<ref name="p1323890713">{{cite news |date=January 13, 1957 |title=Bank Office in Subway Wins Brooklyn Award |page=1C |work=New York Herald Tribune |issn=1941-0646 |id={{ProQuest|1323890713}}}}</ref>

===Track reconfiguration===
{{DeKalb Avenue station (historical layout)|title=Historical track layout}}
The station has been reconfigured a number of times. Originally, the extreme outside tracks in each direction hosted the Fourth Avenue Line local tracks and the next pair hosted the Brighton Line. The middle tracks, which bypassed the station, hosted the Fourth Avenue express tracks. A group of level crossovers at the northern end of the station allowed all tracks access to both sides of the [[Manhattan Bridge]] and to the [[Montague Street Tunnel]]. The Fourth Avenue local tracks led straight onto the Manhattan Bridge west of the station, while the Brighton line tracks led straight to the Montague Street Tunnel, so the crossovers allowed trains from both lines to switch between the bridge and the tunnel.<ref>{{Cite web |last=D'Adamo |first=R. Raleigh |date=1959 |title=DeKalb Avenue Station Reconstruction |url=http://www.thejoekorner.com/lines/dekalb1959.gif |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170807020257/http://www.thejoekorner.com/lines/dekalb1959.gif |archive-date=August 7, 2017 |access-date=May 5, 2017 |website=nycsubway.org}}</ref>

The city government took over the BMT's operations on June 1, 1940.<ref>{{Cite news|date=June 2, 1940|title=B.M.T. Lines Pass to City Ownership; $175,000,000 Deal Completed at City Hall Ceremony-- Mayor 'Motorman No. 1'|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1940/06/02/archives/bmt-lines-pass-to-city-ownership-175000000-deal-completed-at-city.html|access-date=May 14, 2022|issn=0362-4331|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210719094900/https://www.nytimes.com/1940/06/02/archives/bmt-lines-pass-to-city-ownership-175000000-deal-completed-at-city.html|archive-date=July 19, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="p1243059209">{{cite news |date=June 2, 1940 |title=City Takes Over B. M. T. System; Mayor Skippers Midnight Train |page=1 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1243059209}}}}</ref> By then, the original track layout caused many train delays on the Fourth Avenue and Brighton lines. In 1952, the junction was earmarked for "top priority" reconstruction.<ref name="nyt19520606">{{cite news | title=DEKALB REBUILDING GETS TOP PRIORITY; Board of Transportation Says Enlargement of B.M.T. Stop Will Eliminate Bottleneck | newspaper=[[The New York Times]] | date=June 6, 1952 | url=http://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1952/06/06/93374123.html?pageNumber=25 | access-date=July 8, 2016}}</ref> On November 30, 1955, the New York City Transit Authority sent a recommendation to the Board of Estimate for the approval of a $13,152,831 contract to eliminate the bottleneck.<ref name=":0" /> The elimination of the bottleneck was the first step in a larger plan to improve transit service between Brooklyn and Manhattan.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1955/12/01/archives/transit-unit-acts-on-a-bottleneck-asks-estimate-board-to-let.html|title=TRANSIT UNIT ACTS ON A BOTTLENECK; Asks Estimate Board to Let Contract for Track Shifts at De Kalb Ave., Brooklyn|last=Katz|first=Ralph|date=December 1, 1955|work=The New York Times|access-date=March 29, 2018|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="p1327071418">{{cite news |date=December 1, 1955 |title=T. A. to Clear Bottleneck In DeKalb Ave. Subway |page=A10 |work=New York Herald Tribune |issn=1941-0646 |id={{ProQuest|1327071418}}}}</ref> The Citizens Budget Commission recommended that the appropriation be canceled, but three other civic groups opposed the elimination of the funding.<ref>{{Cite news |date=December 15, 1955 |title=3 Civic Groups Fight for DeKalb Project |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1955/12/15/archives/3-civic-groups-fight-for-dekalb-project.html |access-date=May 29, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

[[File:MyrtleAvenueClosing.gif|thumb|left|Myrtle Avenue closing notice]]
During the reconstruction of the junction that started in 1956 and was completed by April 1961, the Brighton Line tracks were connected to the DeKalb Avenue station's outermost tracks. A [[diamond crossover]] north of the station had caused frequent bottlenecks, but was removed during the realignment and replaced with two [[flying junction]]s.<ref name="map"/> All switches immediately north of the station were eliminated. The junction towards the [[Manhattan Bridge]] was rebuilt. To make room for a new flying junction, the [[Myrtle Avenue (BMT Fourth Avenue Line)|Myrtle Avenue station]] was closed. That station's northbound platform remains visible from passing trains, but the southbound platform was demolished to accommodate the new flying junction that replaced the diamond crossover. Platform lengths were doubled to accommodate ten-car trains of {{convert|60|ft|0|adj=mid|-long}} cars.<ref>[http://www.thejoekorner.com/brochures/index-dekalb-esc.html DeKalb Av Escalators]</ref> It was estimated that the reconstruction of the junction increased the junction's train capacity by 25%.<ref name=nyt19520606/>

The [[Chrystie Street Connection]] project was also tied to this improvement, as it resulted in more trains using the bridge, as well as connecting trains to the [[Independent Subway System]] (IND)'s [[IND Sixth Avenue Line|Sixth Avenue Line]] (and thus, to IND lines to [[the Bronx]] and [[Queens]]).<ref name="nyt19520606" /><ref>[http://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1961/04/19/101458878.html "Construction of New IND Tunnel for 6th Ave. Line Begins Today"], ''New York Times'' April 19, 1961; page 41</ref> Over the years, as more of the business community migrated to [[Midtown Manhattan]], the slower tunnel route via [[Lower Manhattan]] became less popular. When the Chrystie Street Connection opened in 1967, the station became a transfer point for trains to and from the IND Sixth Avenue Line.<ref name="nyt-1964-03-01">{{Cite news |date=March 1, 1964 |title=New BMT Tunnel Opens Next Year; Lower East Side Project to Tie In With the IND |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1964/03/01/archives/new-bmt-tunnel-opens-next-year-lower-east-side-project-to-tie-in.html |access-date=May 27, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

=== Station overhauls ===
[[File:DeKalb Avenue station mosaic.jpg|thumb|250px|Station name mosaic|alt=A tile mosaic at the station, with the name "De Kalb Ave" in capital letters.]]
After the 1961 reconstruction, some adjustments were made to the station. The original platform extensions were closed and replaced by straight extensions to the north in 1960. The abandoned portions can be seen from the open platforms and trains. In the mid-1960s, the station platforms were extended northward at least {{convert|150|ft|m}} to accommodate for a {{convert|600|ft|m}} train. It also added new '60s modern look tiling.

The [[Metropolitan Transportation Authority]] (MTA) announced in late 1978 that it would modernize the DeKalb Avenue station. The improvements included new finishes on the walls and floors; acoustical, signage, and lighting improvements; replacement of old mechanical equipment; and new handrails.<ref name="n28723663">{{Cite news |last=Edmonds |first=Richard |date=December 7, 1978 |title=Subway beautification has green light |pages=583 |work=New York Daily News |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/daily-news/28723663/ |access-date=May 25, 2023 |issn=2692-1251 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> In spite of the renovation, the MTA listed the station among the 69 most deteriorated stations in the subway system in 1981.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Gargan|first1=Edward A.|title=AGENCY LISTS ITS 69 MOST DETERIORATED SUBWAY STATIONS|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1981/06/11/nyregion/agency-lists-its-69-most-deteriorated-subway-stations.html|access-date=August 13, 2016|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=June 11, 1981}}</ref>

In the early 2000s, architect [[Lee Harris Pomeroy]] designed a renovation of the DeKalb Avenue station. The plans included adding elevators for [[Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990|ADA accessibility]], as well as restoring the original station tiles and mosaics.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Fried|first=Joseph P.|date=February 3, 2000|title=Untangling Knots in the Subway; Architect's Challenge Is to Make DeKalb Station Flow|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/02/03/nyregion/untangling-knots-subway-architect-s-challenge-make-dekalb-station-flow.html|access-date=May 8, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The latest major overhaul was in 2004–2006. The station was repaired and became an ADA-accessible station. The MTA repaired the staircases, retiled the walls, added new tiles to the floors, upgraded the station's lights and public address system, installed ADA yellow safety treads along the platform edge and replaced the trackbeds for all trains entering or bypassing the station. It also installed elevators on both platforms, as well as elevators to the street level.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://web.mta.info/mta/budget/feb2004-finplan/feb2004-5.pdf |title=MTA Capital Program Information |author=MTA |format=PDF |date=February 2004 |page=16 |access-date=March 28, 2010}}</ref>

==Station layout==
This underground station has six tracks with [[island platform]]s between the two outer pairs of tracks, while the two center tracks bypass the station. B trains stop here on weekdays only except nights, Q and R trains stop here at all times, and D and N trains stop here during nights as well as northbound on weekdays after 6:45&nbsp;p.m. The platform columns are painted red on their lower halves and cream on their upper halves.{{Citation needed|date=May 2023}} The next station to the north is {{stl|NYCS|Jay Street–MetroTech|Fourth}} for Montague Street Tunnel R and late night N trains, {{stl|NYCS|Canal Street|Broadway}} for N and Q trains using the southern tracks of the Manhattan Bridge, and {{stl|NYCS|Grand Street|Sixth}} for {{NYCS Manhattan Bridge north DeKalb|time=nolink}} trains using the northern tracks of the Manhattan Bridge. The next station to the south is {{stl|NYCS|Atlantic Avenue–Barclays Center|Fourth}} on the Fourth Avenue Line for D, N, and R trains and {{stl|NYCS|Atlantic Avenue–Barclays Center|Brighton}} on the Brighton Line for B and Q trains,<ref name=submap>{{NYCS const|map}}</ref> although they are different platforms and formerly different stations.

===Exits===
[[File:DeKalb Avenue-Fleet Street Entrance.JPG|thumb|left|250px|Street stair]]
[[File:DeKalb Avenue; BMT 4th Applebee's Entrance-1.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Elevator and stair outside the now-closed [[Applebee's]] restaurant]]

This station has two entrances/exits, each with access to either the east or west side of Flatbush Avenue. The staffed exit is near the south end and has two staircases and one elevator from each platform that go up to a waiting area above the platforms and tracks that contains two restrooms open from 5:00&nbsp;a.m. to midnight. Outside of the [[turnstile]] bank is a token booth, a single street stair to the southwest corner of DeKalb Avenue and the Flatbush Avenue Extension built inside a store front, and two staircases that meet at their landings and an elevator that go up to the southeast corner outside a former [[Applebee's]] restaurant.<ref name=MTA-DowntownBrooklynMap-2015>{{cite web|title=MTA Neighborhood Maps: Downtown Brooklyn|url=http://web.mta.info/maps/neighborhoods/bkn/B1_downtown_brooklyn_2015.pdf|website=[[Metropolitan Transportation Authority|mta.info]]|publisher=[[Metropolitan Transportation Authority]]|access-date=July 7, 2015|date=2015|archive-date=July 24, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150724175453/http://web.mta.info/maps/neighborhoods/bkn/B1_downtown_brooklyn_2015.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Applebee's Grill + Bar – Brooklyn, New York |url=https://www.yelp.com/biz/applebees-grill-bar-brooklyn-5 |access-date=March 4, 2022 |website=Yelp |language=en-US}}</ref> The centers of the platforms have a crossover that connects them both. Both the crossover and the staffed exit were part of a wide mezzanine area, but most of the mezzanine was closed off and converted to crew rooms.

The other entrance/exit is at the station's extreme north end and is unstaffed. An up-only escalator and long staircase from each platform goes up to a mezzanine above the tracks. Two pairs of exit-only turnstiles and one set of four turnstiles provide entrance/exit from the system. This entrance has two street stairs: one to Fleet Street on the east side, outside [[Long Island University Brooklyn]], and the other to the former [[Albee Square]] on the west side, outside the [[City Point (Brooklyn)|City Point]] development.<ref name=MTA-DowntownBrooklynMap-2015/>
[[File:DeKalb Avenue art vc.jpg|thumb|Detail of ''DeKalb Improvisation'' artwork]]
Both fare control areas feature a 2005 artwork called ''DeKalb Improvisation'' by Stephen Johnson. It consists of a large mural in the main fare control area and several smaller ones in the secondary one.

===Track layout===
North of the station, the outer and bypass tracks head towards the [[Manhattan Bridge]] to [[Manhattan]] with a [[flying junction]]. B and D trains use the north side of the bridge via the [[Chrystie Street Connection]] to the [[IND Sixth Avenue Line]]. N and Q trains use the south side of the bridge to the [[BMT Broadway Line]].<ref name="map" /> Trains traveling through this interlocking are frequently delayed, since Brighton and Fourth Avenue trains have to cross over each other to use both the Broadway and Sixth Avenue Lines.<ref name="Guse 2022">{{cite web |last=Guse |first=Clayton |date=May 8, 2022 |title=and MTA has no fix in sight |url=https://www.nydailynews.com/new-york/ny-mta-subway-bottleneck-dekalb-interlocking-20220508-uq4sxpr7efdllfxyyvuqlhgjp4-story.html |access-date=May 8, 2022 |website=New York Daily News}}</ref> R trains and late night N trains continue on the middle tracks north along the [[BMT Fourth Avenue Line]] into the [[Montague Street Tunnel]] towards the BMT Broadway Line.

South of the station, the bypass tracks become the express tracks on the Fourth Avenue Line. The four remaining tracks become six tracks at a [[flying junction]].<ref name="map">[http://images.nycsubway.org/trackmap/detail-jaydklb.png Detailed view of current track layout]
*See also: {{NYCS const|trackref|469}}</ref> Trains headed south on the tunnel local tracks or outer tracks proceed to the [[BMT Brighton Line]] or switch from those two tracks and provide the route to the Fourth Avenue Line local tracks. In the current service pattern, the tunnel route is not used for Brighton Line trains.<ref name="map"/> Also south of this station, there is a [[Bellmouth (railroad terminology)|bellmouth]] where the northbound track of the Brighton Line curves northwest onto Fulton Street. The bellmouth marks the location where the westbound track of the proposed Ashland Place Connection, which would have connected to the now-demolished [[Fulton Street Line (elevated)|BMT Fulton Street El]], would have merged with the Brighton Line.<ref name="n125269328">{{Cite news |date=January 31, 1916 |title=Citizens Win Transit Victory |pages=1 |work=The Brooklyn Citizen |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-citizen-citizens-win-transi/125269328/ |access-date=May 25, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref><ref name="n125269450">{{Cite news |date=February 19, 1917 |title=P.S.C. Asks Passage of Fulton "L" Link |pages=18 |work=The Brooklyn Daily Eagle |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-daily-eagle-psc-asks-pas/125269450/ |access-date=May 25, 2023 |via=newspapers.com}}</ref> The eastbound track of the Ashland Place Connection was not built. The track would have diverged from the southbound Brighton Line between Ashland Place and St. Felix Street, then curved east under Lafayette Avenue, to avoid an at-grade junction with the Brighton Line.<ref name="n125269450" />

The DeKalb Avenue station was also built with provisions for a possible track connection to [[Nevins Street (IRT Eastern Parkway Line)|Nevins Street]] station. The station has a shortened [[mezzanine (architecture)|mezzanine]] because room was needed for a proposed Lafayette Avenue line. The subway connection was never built. North of this station, near the [[Manhattan Bridge]], there is a provision for a never-built loop back to southern Brooklyn without crossing the Manhattan Bridge into Manhattan. [[Bellmouth (railroad terminology)|Bellmouths]] for the unbuilt loop are visible from passing trains. South of this station, a junction was built at Fulton Street for a never-built branch to run via Lafayette Avenue and Broadway.<ref name="map"/>

